A work does not have to live in one file. A book can keep each chapter in its
own file, a long article can pull its methods and results from separate sources,
and a website is a set of pages by definition. Enscribe stitches all of them into
one document at build time — and a single rule keeps the result predictable.

# Why split a document across files

An author reaches for multiple files for the ordinary reasons of scale: a book or
a long report is easier to draft, navigate, and revise when each chapter is its
own file rather than one enormous scroll, and a file is a natural unit of work —
one to write, hand to a co-author, or set aside. The same instinct enables reuse:
a shared preface, a standard methods section, or a boilerplate appendix can be
pulled into more than one document, or into more than one place in the same one.
Enscribe serves all of these with one small vocabulary, built on a single idea.

# The one rule: pasted in place

External content is **spliced in at its call site, as if you had typed it there.**
That is the whole model. Assembly is a *textual* step that runs first — it answers
"what is the text?" — and only then does enscribe work out the structure: which
section opens where, and what numbers as what. The file boundaries are invisible to
everything downstream, so an assembled document means exactly what the same words
would mean typed into one file.

The consequence worth internalising is what happens to the text *after* a
reference. Here a master sources two sections and types a line of its own between
them:

<code-block #code:mf-model-1>
<section src=section1.emd | Section 1>

Random text

<section src=section2.emd | Section 2>
</code-block>

*(Source only — a sealed preview box cannot reach out to other files. The
equivalent single file below renders live.)*

*Section 1*'s content is `section1.emd`'s text **followed by** `Random text`: the
line in between belongs to the section above it, because that section is still open
when the line is read. *Section 2* then peer-closes *Section 1*, exactly as a second
`<section>` always closes the first. Paste the two children in place and this is the
single file the master is equivalent to — and it renders the same way:

<minipage #mp:mf-model-1>
<section | Section 1>
The first section, from its own file.

Random text

<section | Section 2>
The second section, from its own file.
</minipage>

*The interstitial "Random text" sits inside Section 1, not between the two sections
— assembly changes nothing about how the words are read.*

# The structural forms

Every structural element that holds content accepts a `src`, and the child file
supplies that element's **initial** content. In an article, sections:

<code-block #code:mf-section>
<section src=intro.emd | Introduction>
</code-block>

In a book, chapters — and prefaces, appendices, and parts — source the same way:

<code-block #code:mf-chapter>
<chapter src=origins.emd | Origins>
<chapter src=populations.emd | Modern populations>
</code-block>

In a website, a nav entry names its page's file:

<code-block #code:mf-item>
<nav>
<item src=home.emd | Home>
<item src=about.emd | About>
</nav>
</code-block>

*(Source only — these forms assemble across real files, which a sealed preview box
cannot do.)*

All three behave identically. The entry opens the element, splices the child as its
initial content, and **stays open** — so anything you type after it, up to the next
peer, belongs to it, by the pasted-in-place rule above. The pipe title overrides the
child file's own title; drop the pipe and the child's `<title>` is used instead.

# `<include>`: splice anything, anywhere

The structural `src` forms are shorthand for one primitive: `<include>`. An
`<include>` splices a file at its own position and does nothing else — it opens no
element, closes none, and adds no structure. Reach for it to break prose into files
without wrapping each piece in a section:

<code-block #code:mf-include>
Opening prose, in the master.

<include src=methods.emd />

Prose between the includes reads normally.

<include src=results/tables.emd />

Closing prose.
</code-block>

Each file drops in at its spot and the whole reads as one continuous document. An
included file may itself include others, to any depth.

The one topology enscribe will not follow is a **cycle** — a file that includes
itself, directly or through a chain that leads back to it. Rather than loop forever,
the offending include renders a flagged marker in place, naming the cycle, and
assembly carries on. This is what a reader sees on the page:

> `??include cycle: master.emd → intro.emd → master.emd??`

*(Quoted, not previewed: a live cycle needs real files that point back at each
other, which a sealed box cannot host. The marker is enscribe's always-renders
habit — a failure is shown, never silent.)*

Including the same file at two *different* places is not a cycle and is perfectly
legal — it reads exactly as if its text were typed at both spots.

# Paths, and one document across many pages

**A path is relative to the file that writes it** — resolved against the directory
of the *including* file, not the master. Each file names its children the way it
sits on disk, so a moved subtree keeps working. In a master beside a `chapters/`
folder:

<code-block #code:mf-path-1>
<chapter src=chapters/origins.emd | Origins>
</code-block>

and inside `chapters/origins.emd`, beside its own `figures/` folder:

<code-block #code:mf-path-2>
<include src=figures/skull.emd />
</code-block>

This is the same rule assets and per-chapter bibliographies already follow — one
resolution rule everywhere, and the intuition of LaTeX's `\input`.

**You author once; how the document is delivered does not change where things land.**
The same assembled document renders as one page or splits into per-chapter pages,
and every piece keeps its place either way: an interlude you type between two
chapters renders with the chapter it belongs to, a figure in that interlude numbers
within that chapter, and a cross-reference to it resolves wherever the reader is.
Splitting a document into files and splitting a rendering into pages are independent
choices — assembly reconciles them so you don't have to.

# Going deeper

This chapter is the common path. The parts an author rarely has to think about —
how cross-references find their target across files, how each page earns its URL,
how a library in every file merges into one bibliography — are handled for you, and
specified in full elsewhere:

- The **Design** page's *Multi-file documents and transclusion* section — the model
  and the reasoning behind it.
- `notes/specs/master-document.md` — the complete specification: assembly order,
  numbering across files, the shared citation registry, and cross-reference
  resolution.
- `notes/specs/website.md` — how a website's pages compose, each numbering in its
  own right over one shared cross-reference registry.
